  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the domain of our function is $x\\in [-4,4].$ \n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ does not pass the horizontal line test on the interval $x\\in (-1,1).$ In this case, the horizontal line $y=1$ intersects the graph at more than one point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14229037/assets/d295d104bd8340a8701327e19103bfa8fd12622f1500c405411d2afd8bd47801.png)\n\n- Among the given options, the largest domain on which the function $f(x)$ is invertible is $x\\in [-4,-1)\\cup [0,4].$ On this domain, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14229037/assets/a74a9a3f1fe52a7c841c763ccffb51faa922fdaec56825ef1000b5becc3534fe.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x\\in [-4,-1)\\cup [0,4].$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
